```

Note: req->from and req->len are uint64_t. `(req->from + TREEPAGESIZE - 1)` is computed as uint64_t, then `& ~((off_t)TREEPAGESIZE - 1)` — mixing uint64_t and off_t (long): the usual arithmetic conversions → unsigned long. Assigning to off_t is fine as long as it's within range (range checks have been done). Cleaner approach: first cast to off_t:

```c
		off_t from = (off_t)req->from;
		off_t len = (off_t)req->len;
		off_t pos = (from + TREEPAGESIZE - 1) & ~((off_t)TREEPAGESIZE - 1);
		off_t end = (from + len) & ~((off_t)TREEPAGESIZE - 1);
```

Edge case: if the trim range is smaller than a block → pos can end up greater than end (e.g., from=100, len=100: pos=4096, end=0) → the loop doesn't execute. No problem.

